There's no existing code to score, so below are the risks that shaped the design (priority-tagged), then the code. I'm assuming Spring Boot 3.x with Spring AI 1.1.x (`OllamaChatOptions`); in 1.0.x the class is `OllamaOptions`.

## Main risks

- **[P0] Silent context truncation.** Ollama's default `num_ctx` is small (2048-4096). 2000 records is roughly 60-100k tokens, so the model would see only part of your data, with no error. Set `num_ctx` explicitly and chunk the input.
- **[P0] Prompt injection.** Record fields are untrusted text. The prompt must mark them as data only.
- **[P1] Counting and summing in the LLM.** Models are unreliable at exact aggregation over hundreds of rows. Compute totals and counts in Java and pass them in as facts (see the note at the end).
- **[P1] Qwen3 `<think>` output.** It pollutes your markdown. Disable thinking, and strip the tags defensively.
- **[P1] HTTP timeouts.** A 27B model on long input takes minutes, so the default read timeout will fail.
- **[P2] Concurrency.** A single GPU serializes requests anyway, so bound parallelism (and set `OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL` on the server if you want more than 1).

## Config

```properties
spring.ai.ollama.base-url=http://localhost:11434
spring.ai.ollama.chat.options.model=<your exact tag from `ollama list`>
spring.ai.ollama.chat.options.temperature=0.2
spring.ai.ollama.chat.options.num-ctx=32768
analysis.chunk-chars=36000
analysis.max-records=2000
analysis.parallelism=2
```

I couldn't verify a `qwen3.8-27b` tag. Use whatever `ollama list` shows, and keep `num-ctx` within what that model supports.

## Code

```java
@Configuration
class AiConfig {

    // Long generations: raise the read timeout on the RestClient Spring AI uses
    @Bean
    RestClientCustomizer ollamaTimeouts() {
        HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
                .connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10)).build();
        JdkClientHttpRequestFactory factory = new JdkClientHttpRequestFactory(client);
        factory.setReadTimeout(Duration.ofMinutes(10));
        return builder -> builder.requestFactory(factory);
    }

    @Bean
    ChatClient chatClient(ChatClient.Builder builder) {
        return builder
                .defaultOptions(OllamaChatOptions.builder()
                        .numCtx(32768)
                        .temperature(0.2)
                        .disableThinking()   // Qwen3 think mode off
                        .build())
                .build();
    }
}
